{"reason": "Based on the current execution results, the model achieves a validation log-loss of 0.5728, close to the initial plan's baseline of 0.57. The initial plan proposes three changes: Transformer encoder (model_design), Focal Loss (training_evaluation), and Cosine schedule with restarts (training_evaluation). The historical experiment successfully integrated a BiLSTM with attention and stylometric features, achieving a similar log-loss. To refine and optimize, I will focus on model_design and training_evaluation because these are where the proposed modifications directly improve performance. The data processing module is already effective, and the historical experiment used similar feature engineering; no changes are needed there. The model_design change (Transformer encoder) is promising but complex, and the training_evaluation changes (Focal Loss, Cosine schedule) are easier to implement and have synergy. I will keep the data processing unchanged to avoid unnecessary complexity.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the WordBiLSTMEncoder with a TransformerEncoder (nn.TransformerEncoder with 4 layers, 8 heads, d_model=256, dropout=0.3, and learned positional embeddings). The embedding layer remains, but the LSTM is replaced by the transformer. This captures long-range dependencies better for author stylistic patterns. Historical experiments show that transformers outperform LSTMs on text classification by 5-10% log-loss improvement. Implementation: define a TransformerWordEncoder class with positional encoding and a transformer encoder, outputting the mean of the encoder's output (or the CLS token if added). Keep the same interface (input: word_ids, mask; output: context vector of size 256). Also adjust the classifier input dimension accordingly.", "training_evaluation": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) and replace the LinearLR scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2, eta_min=1e-6). Focal Loss mitigates class imbalance by down-weighting easy examples, improving minority class performance. Cosine schedule with restarts helps escape local minima and generalize better. Implementation: define focal_loss function; modify criterion in training loop. For scheduler, after 5 epochs of linear warmup, switch to CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts and remove the step in the linear schedule. Keep other hyperparameters (batch size, dropout, etc.) unchanged as per the initial plan."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Based on the current execution results, the model achieves a validation log-loss of 0.5728, close to the initial plan's baseline of 0.57. The initial plan proposes three changes: Transformer encoder (model_design), Focal Loss (training_evaluation), and Cosine schedule with restarts (training_evaluation). The historical experiment successfully integrated a BiLSTM with attention and stylometric features, achieving a similar log-loss. To refine and optimize, I will focus on model_design and training_evaluation because these are where the proposed modifications directly improve performance. The data processing module is already effective, and the historical experiment used similar feature engineering; no changes are needed there. The model_design change (Transformer encoder) is promising but complex, and the training_evaluation changes (Focal Loss, Cosine schedule) are easier to implement and have synergy. I will keep the data processing unchanged to avoid unnecessary complexity.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the WordBiLSTMEncoder with a TransformerEncoder (nn.TransformerEncoder with 4 layers, 8 heads, d_model=256, dropout=0.3, and learned positional embeddings). The embedding layer remains, but the LSTM is replaced by the transformer. This captures long-range dependencies better for author stylistic patterns. Historical experiments show that transformers outperform LSTMs on text classification by 5-10% log-loss improvement. Implementation: define a TransformerWordEncoder class with positional encoding and a transformer encoder, outputting the mean of the encoder's output (or the CLS token if added). Keep the same interface (input: word_ids, mask; output: context vector of size 256). Also adjust the classifier input dimension accordingly.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) and replace the LinearLR scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2, eta_min=1e-6). Focal Loss mitigates class imbalance by down-weighting easy examples, improving minority class performance. Cosine schedule with restarts helps escape local minima and generalize better. Implementation: define focal_loss function; modify criterion in training loop. For scheduler, after 5 epochs of linear warmup, switch to CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts and remove the step in the linear schedule. Keep other hyperparameters (batch size, dropout, etc.) unchanged as per the initial plan."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current validation log loss of 0.5396 shows that the transformer word encoder is a significant improvement over the historical LSTM baseline (0.5728). However, the model still plateaus, indicating that data processing and feature engineering (especially subword tokenization and additional stylometric features) can enhance performance, as seen in successful historical experiments. The training_evaluation module also benefits from refined scheduling based on historical practices (using cosine annealing only after warmup). I choose model_design and training_evaluation because data processing is already effective and historical experiments suggest that further feature engineering yields diminishing returns; the main bottleneck is the word encoder's adaptation and training stability.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current TransformerWordEncoder with a pretrained DistilRoBERTa encoder from transformers library. Specifically, load 'distilroberta-base', freeze the first 4 transformer layers, and fine-tune the top 2 layers along with a newly added classification head. The encoder will output the pooled representation (CLS token) of size 768. Then, project it to 256 dimensions via a linear layer to match the existing concatenation dimension. This change leverages pretrained knowledge to better capture rare author-specific terms (e.g., 'eldritch') and syntax, addressing the initial plan's root cause of insufficient training data for the word encoder. Implementation: define a DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder class that loads the pretrained model, freezes initial layers, and adds a projection layer. Keep the same input interface: tokenize text using the DistilRoBERTa tokenizer (max_length=128, truncation, padding) to produce input_ids and attention_mask; output a 256-d context vector. Ensure consistent variable names (e.g., 'word_ids' becomes 'input_ids', 'word_mask' becomes 'attention_mask') and update the forward call accordingly.", "training_evaluation": "Refine the learning rate schedule: use a linear warmup for the first 10% of total steps (as in initial plan) followed by linear decay instead of cosine annealing, because historical experiments with cosine annealing led to earlier plateaus. Set initial learning rate to 1e-5 (lower for pretrained model) with weight decay 0.01. Keep Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) but add label smoothing (epsilon=0.1) to improve calibration. Monitor validation log loss and use early stopping with patience 7. This change stabilizes fine-tuning of the pretrained encoder, preventing catastrophic forgetting, and the linear decay provides smoother convergence. Implementation: modify optimizer to use AdamW with learning rate 1e-5, add label smoothing to FocalLoss by integrating it into cross-entropy computation, and replace the cosine scheduler with get_linear_schedule_with_warmup from transformers. Keep batch size, gradient clipping, and AMP unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current validation log loss of 0.5396 shows that the transformer word encoder is a significant improvement over the historical LSTM baseline (0.5728). However, the model still plateaus, indicating that data processing and feature engineering (especially subword tokenization and additional stylometric features) can enhance performance, as seen in successful historical experiments. The training_evaluation module also benefits from refined scheduling based on historical practices (using cosine annealing only after warmup). I choose model_design and training_evaluation because data processing is already effective and historical experiments suggest that further feature engineering yields diminishing returns; the main bottleneck is the word encoder's adaptation and training stability.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current TransformerWordEncoder with a pretrained DistilRoBERTa encoder from transformers library. Specifically, load 'distilroberta-base', freeze the first 4 transformer layers, and fine-tune the top 2 layers along with a newly added classification head. The encoder will output the pooled representation (CLS token) of size 768. Then, project it to 256 dimensions via a linear layer to match the existing concatenation dimension. This change leverages pretrained knowledge to better capture rare author-specific terms (e.g., 'eldritch') and syntax, addressing the initial plan's root cause of insufficient training data for the word encoder. Implementation: define a DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder class that loads the pretrained model, freezes initial layers, and adds a projection layer. Keep the same input interface: tokenize text using the DistilRoBERTa tokenizer (max_length=128, truncation, padding) to produce input_ids and attention_mask; output a 256-d context vector. Ensure consistent variable names (e.g., 'word_ids' becomes 'input_ids', 'word_mask' becomes 'attention_mask') and update the forward call accordingly.",
    "training_evaluation": "Refine the learning rate schedule: use a linear warmup for the first 10% of total steps (as in initial plan) followed by linear decay instead of cosine annealing, because historical experiments with cosine annealing led to earlier plateaus. Set initial learning rate to 1e-5 (lower for pretrained model) with weight decay 0.01. Keep Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) but add label smoothing (epsilon=0.1) to improve calibration. Monitor validation log loss and use early stopping with patience 7. This change stabilizes fine-tuning of the pretrained encoder, preventing catastrophic forgetting, and the linear decay provides smoother convergence. Implementation: modify optimizer to use AdamW with learning rate 1e-5, add label smoothing to FocalLoss by integrating it into cross-entropy computation, and replace the cosine scheduler with get_linear_schedule_with_warmup from transformers. Keep batch size, gradient clipping, and AMP unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show a training loss of 0.047 and validation loss of 0.493, confirming overfitting and underutilization of complementary features. The historical experiment successfully replaced a BiLSTM with a Transformer encoder and improved performance, suggesting that more sophisticated fusion can help. The initial plan's core direction is to fix the naive concatenation fusion and overfitting in DistilRoBERTa. I will focus on model_design (cross-modal attention fusion) and training_evaluation (stochastic depth) because these directly address the identified root causes. Data processing remains effective and unchanged.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of char, word, and stylometric features with a cross-modal multi-head attention fusion module. Specifically, create a CrossModalAttention class that takes three feature vectors (each of dimension d=256) and applies multi-head attention (4 heads, d_k=64) where each modality serves as query to attend over the other two. The attended features are concatenated and passed through a final projection layer to produce a fused representation of size 256. This replaces the current concat operation. The CharCNNEncoder output dimension must be fixed to 256 via a linear projection, and the StylometricEncoder output dimension is already 64, so add a linear projection to 256. The word encoder already outputs 256. Ensure the forward method signature remains unchanged: (char_ids, input_ids, attention_mask, stylometric_features) -> logits.", "training_evaluation": "Apply stochastic depth to the fine-tuned DistilRoBERTa layers (layers 4 and 5). During training, for each forward pass, randomly drop layers 4 and 5 with survival probabilities p4=0.9 and p5=0.8 respectively; during inference, keep all layers. Implement this by subclassing the forward method of DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder: after obtaining the base model outputs, apply a mask to the hidden states before the final layer. Since the model uses HuggingFace's AutoModel, access encoder.layer[4] and [5] outputs and apply stochastic depth accordingly. This requires overriding the forward method or using a hook. Keep all other training hyperparameters (FocalLoss, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged as in the initial plan."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show a training loss of 0.047 and validation loss of 0.493, confirming overfitting and underutilization of complementary features. The historical experiment successfully replaced a BiLSTM with a Transformer encoder and improved performance, suggesting that more sophisticated fusion can help. The initial plan's core direction is to fix the naive concatenation fusion and overfitting in DistilRoBERTa. I will focus on model_design (cross-modal attention fusion) and training_evaluation (stochastic depth) because these directly address the identified root causes. Data processing remains effective and unchanged.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of char, word, and stylometric features with a cross-modal multi-head attention fusion module. Specifically, create a CrossModalAttention class that takes three feature vectors (each of dimension d=256) and applies multi-head attention (4 heads, d_k=64) where each modality serves as query to attend over the other two. The attended features are concatenated and passed through a final projection layer to produce a fused representation of size 256. This replaces the current concat operation. The CharCNNEncoder output dimension must be fixed to 256 via a linear projection, and the StylometricEncoder output dimension is already 64, so add a linear projection to 256. The word encoder already outputs 256. Ensure the forward method signature remains unchanged: (char_ids, input_ids, attention_mask, stylometric_features) -> logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "Apply stochastic depth to the fine-tuned DistilRoBERTa layers (layers 4 and 5). During training, for each forward pass, randomly drop layers 4 and 5 with survival probabilities p4=0.9 and p5=0.8 respectively; during inference, keep all layers. Implement this by subclassing the forward method of DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder: after obtaining the base model outputs, apply a mask to the hidden states before the final layer. Since the model uses HuggingFace's AutoModel, access encoder.layer[4] and [5] outputs and apply stochastic depth accordingly. This requires overriding the forward method or using a hook. Keep all other training hyperparameters (FocalLoss, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged as in the initial plan."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan aims to reduce overfitting via multi-sample dropout and layer-wise learning rate decay. The execution results show clear overfitting: training loss drops to 0.055 while validation loss plateaus at 0.464. Historical experiments successfully used stochastic depth and differential learning rates to combat similar overfitting patterns. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules to implement these changes. Data processing remains adequate and unchanged.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Add multi-sample dropout with K=4 to DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder. During forward pass, compute K independent dropout masks on the projected embeddings (after projection and before layer_norm), average them, and then apply layer_norm. The single dropout is replaced by an ensemble-in-training approach. Additionally, implement stochastic depth for the fine-tuned top two transformer layers (layers 4 and 5) with survival probabilities p4=0.9 and p5=0.8. Both modifications require overriding the forward method to capture intermediate hidden states from the base model and apply the masks. Interface remains: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) -> tensor of shape [batch, 256].", "training_evaluation": "Implement layer-wise learning rate decay for the DistilRoBERTa encoder. Group parameters by layer: for layer i in the unfrozen layers (4 and 5), set lr = base_lr * 0.8^(N-i) where N is number of unfrozen layers (here N=2). For other parameters (CharCNN, StylometricEncoder, classifier), use the base learning rate. Use AdamW optimizer with separate parameter groups, keeping the same scheduler (linear warmup with linear decay) and FocalLoss. This preserves all other training hyperparameters."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan aims to reduce overfitting via multi-sample dropout and layer-wise learning rate decay. The execution results show clear overfitting: training loss drops to 0.055 while validation loss plateaus at 0.464. Historical experiments successfully used stochastic depth and differential learning rates to combat similar overfitting patterns. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules to implement these changes. Data processing remains adequate and unchanged.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Add multi-sample dropout with K=4 to DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder. During forward pass, compute K independent dropout masks on the projected embeddings (after projection and before layer_norm), average them, and then apply layer_norm. The single dropout is replaced by an ensemble-in-training approach. Additionally, implement stochastic depth for the fine-tuned top two transformer layers (layers 4 and 5) with survival probabilities p4=0.9 and p5=0.8. Both modifications require overriding the forward method to capture intermediate hidden states from the base model and apply the masks. Interface remains: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) -> tensor of shape [batch, 256].",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement layer-wise learning rate decay for the DistilRoBERTa encoder. Group parameters by layer: for layer i in the unfrozen layers (4 and 5), set lr = base_lr * 0.8^(N-i) where N is number of unfrozen layers (here N=2). For other parameters (CharCNN, StylometricEncoder, classifier), use the base learning rate. Use AdamW optimizer with separate parameter groups, keeping the same scheduler (linear warmup with linear decay) and FocalLoss. This preserves all other training hyperparameters."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss of 0.5017 and accuracy of 0.8349, with clear overfitting (training loss drops to 0.0441 while validation plateaus). The initial plan targets this overfitting by replacing FocalLoss with cross-entropy + label smoothing, reducing weight_decay, adding layer-wise attention pooling, and TTA. Historical experiments show that cross-entropy with label smoothing and reduced weight_decay (from 0.01 to 0.001) directly addresses overfitting and improves calibration. Layer-wise attention pooling is a more sophisticated way to utilize all transformer layers than the multi-sample dropout and stochastic depth that failed in past experiments (where they hurt convergence). TTA is orthogonally beneficial as shown in historical successes. Data processing is already effective and unchanged. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation to implement these changes.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder's frozen lower layers with a trainable head that uses a learnable weighted combination of all 6 transformer layers (layer-wise attention pooling) instead of only using the last hidden state's CLS token, and remove the stochastic depth on layers 4-5. Specifically, in the forward method, obtain all hidden states (tuple of 7 tensors), stack the last 6 layer outputs (indices 1-6) to shape [batch, seq_len, 768, 6], apply mean pooling over seq_len to get [batch, 768, 6], then use a learnable attention mechanism (nn.Linear(768,1) followed by softmax over layers) to compute weighted sum, resulting in [batch, 768]. Add a projection to 256 and layer_norm. This replaces the CLS token extraction and stochastic depth. Preserve the interface: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) -> tensor of shape [batch, 256].", "training_evaluation": "Replace FocalLoss (gamma=2.0) with standard cross-entropy loss with label_smoothing=0.1, and reduce weight_decay from 0.01 to 0.001. Also remove the layer-wise learning rate decay (use uniform LR for all trainable parameters), as historical experiments suggest it complicates optimization unnecessarily when full fine-tuning is applied. Add test-time augmentation (TTA) by averaging predictions between original text and punctuation-stripped text (remove commas, semicolons, colons, dashes, quotes, exclamation marks, question marks, keeping periods and apostrophes) during inference. Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged: optimizer (AdamW with eps=1e-8), scheduler (linear warmup 10% + linear decay), batch size (32/64), max epochs (100), patience (7), gradient clip (1.0)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss of 0.5017 and accuracy of 0.8349, with clear overfitting (training loss drops to 0.0441 while validation plateaus). The initial plan targets this overfitting by replacing FocalLoss with cross-entropy + label smoothing, reducing weight_decay, adding layer-wise attention pooling, and TTA. Historical experiments show that cross-entropy with label smoothing and reduced weight_decay (from 0.01 to 0.001) directly addresses overfitting and improves calibration. Layer-wise attention pooling is a more sophisticated way to utilize all transformer layers than the multi-sample dropout and stochastic depth that failed in past experiments (where they hurt convergence). TTA is orthogonally beneficial as shown in historical successes. Data processing is already effective and unchanged. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation to implement these changes.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the DistilRoBERTaWordEncoder's frozen lower layers with a trainable head that uses a learnable weighted combination of all 6 transformer layers (layer-wise attention pooling) instead of only using the last hidden state's CLS token, and remove the stochastic depth on layers 4-5. Specifically, in the forward method, obtain all hidden states (tuple of 7 tensors), stack the last 6 layer outputs (indices 1-6) to shape [batch, seq_len, 768, 6], apply mean pooling over seq_len to get [batch, 768, 6], then use a learnable attention mechanism (nn.Linear(768,1) followed by softmax over layers) to compute weighted sum, resulting in [batch, 768]. Add a projection to 256 and layer_norm. This replaces the CLS token extraction and stochastic depth. Preserve the interface: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) -> tensor of shape [batch, 256].",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace FocalLoss (gamma=2.0) with standard cross-entropy loss with label_smoothing=0.1, and reduce weight_decay from 0.01 to 0.001. Also remove the layer-wise learning rate decay (use uniform LR for all trainable parameters), as historical experiments suggest it complicates optimization unnecessarily when full fine-tuning is applied. Add test-time augmentation (TTA) by averaging predictions between original text and punctuation-stripped text (remove commas, semicolons, colons, dashes, quotes, exclamation marks, question marks, keeping periods and apostrophes) during inference. Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged: optimizer (AdamW with eps=1e-8), scheduler (linear warmup 10% + linear decay), batch size (32/64), max epochs (100), patience (7), gradient clip (1.0)."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting: DeBERTa validation loss increases from 0.4018 (epoch 3) to 0.8718 on the final evaluation, and the ensemble weight optimization allocates only 10% to DeBERTa, indicating weak transformer features. To address this, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules. Model_design changes align with the initial plan's suggestion to replace DeBERTa-large with base and add attention pooling, which similar historical experiments (successful use of Transformer encoder with attention) show improves feature extraction. Training_evaluation modifications incorporate 5-fold CV and MixUp from the initial plan; similar historical experiments demonstrate that CV reduces variance and MixUp regularizes effectively, while avoiding the failed multi-sample dropout approach that did not prevent overfitting. Data processing remains adequate and unchanged.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace microsoft/deberta-v3-large with microsoft/deberta-v3-base and add a trainable weighted attention pooling layer on top of the entire sequence (not just CLS token). Implement a custom DebertaWithAttentionPooling class that passes token embeddings through a 2-layer MLP (768->256->1) to compute attention weights, then computes a weighted sum over non-padded token embeddings to produce a 768-dimensional pooled embedding. This reduces model capacity to mitigate overfitting and captures richer positional information, as similar successful experiments used Transformer encoders with attention for better feature extraction. Keep the same tokenizer (deberta-v3-large tokenizer works) and interface: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) returns logits.", "training_evaluation": "Replace single train/val split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for both feature extraction and model training, and add MixUp data augmentation (alpha=0.2) during DeBERTa training. Implement StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, random_state=42; for each fold, train a separate DeBERTa-base+attention-pooling model with MixUp (sample λ ~ Beta(0.2,0.2), mix embedding-layer outputs, loss = λ*CE(original) + (1-λ)*CE(shuffled)), then extract pooled embeddings for held-out and test data. Train independent XGBoost and Logistic Regression per fold, and average test predictions across folds. Use reduced learning rate 1e-5 and early stopping patience=7. This reduces variance and regularizes the transformer, similar to successful historical experiments with CV and data augmentation, while avoiding the failed multi-sample dropout that did not stabilize training."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting: DeBERTa validation loss increases from 0.4018 (epoch 3) to 0.8718 on the final evaluation, and the ensemble weight optimization allocates only 10% to DeBERTa, indicating weak transformer features. To address this, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation modules. Model_design changes align with the initial plan's suggestion to replace DeBERTa-large with base and add attention pooling, which similar historical experiments (successful use of Transformer encoder with attention) show improves feature extraction. Training_evaluation modifications incorporate 5-fold CV and MixUp from the initial plan; similar historical experiments demonstrate that CV reduces variance and MixUp regularizes effectively, while avoiding the failed multi-sample dropout approach that did not prevent overfitting. Data processing remains adequate and unchanged.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace microsoft/deberta-v3-large with microsoft/deberta-v3-base and add a trainable weighted attention pooling layer on top of the entire sequence (not just CLS token). Implement a custom DebertaWithAttentionPooling class that passes token embeddings through a 2-layer MLP (768->256->1) to compute attention weights, then computes a weighted sum over non-padded token embeddings to produce a 768-dimensional pooled embedding. This reduces model capacity to mitigate overfitting and captures richer positional information, as similar successful experiments used Transformer encoders with attention for better feature extraction. Keep the same tokenizer (deberta-v3-large tokenizer works) and interface: forward(input_ids, attention_mask) returns logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace single train/val split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for both feature extraction and model training, and add MixUp data augmentation (alpha=0.2) during DeBERTa training. Implement StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, random_state=42; for each fold, train a separate DeBERTa-base+attention-pooling model with MixUp (sample λ ~ Beta(0.2,0.2), mix embedding-layer outputs, loss = λ*CE(original) + (1-λ)*CE(shuffled)), then extract pooled embeddings for held-out and test data. Train independent XGBoost and Logistic Regression per fold, and average test predictions across folds. Use reduced learning rate 1e-5 and early stopping patience=7. This reduces variance and regularizes the transformer, similar to successful historical experiments with CV and data augmentation, while avoiding the failed multi-sample dropout that did not stabilize training."
  }
}"}

